WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.640
Bruce Willis is iconic as futuristic cab driver&nbsp;
Korben Dallas in "The Fifth Element" — but what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.800
if a certain "Lethal Weapon" star was&nbsp;
cast instead? From a rock star to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:14.720
"Pretty Woman," here are some stars who&nbsp;
nearly appeared in the sci-fi classic. 
 &nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.840
In an alternate universe, Prince would have&nbsp;
played the role of Ruby Rhod. It's a casting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.440
decision that's pretty much perfect, especially&nbsp;
because Rhod exhibits the eccentricity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.280
sheer swagger that the "Purple Rain"&nbsp;
artist already possessed. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.040
director Luc Besson said that he wrote&nbsp;
the Ruby Rhod character specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.600
for the Grammy-winning artist.&nbsp;
Besson wrote in a Reddit AMA:

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.840
"It was written for Prince. I&nbsp;
met him, he said yes. But then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.920
he went on a world tour for 10 years!!&nbsp;
We couldn't find time in his schedule."

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.920
In a Twitter post from 2016, Besson even&nbsp;
shared Jean Paul Gaultier's concept art&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.280
of Prince in the role. The famed costume&nbsp;
designer began working with Prince early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.720
on in the film's production, though he&nbsp;
found himself butting heads with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.160
artist. As part of The Brooklyn Museum's&nbsp;
"The Fashion World of Jean Paul Gaultier:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:03.560
From the Sidewalk to the Catwalk" exhibit,&nbsp;
Gaultier said this about the singer:

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.800
"I showed him my drawings, but he&nbsp;
didn't say a word. [...] Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.920
Luc told me that Prince had been&nbsp;
very surprised and amused — by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.280
my presentation, but that he found&nbsp;
the costumes a bit too effeminate."

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:18.160
Ultimately, the role of Ruby Rhod went&nbsp;
to Chris Tucker and the rest is history.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.080
In a separate Reddit AMA, director Luc&nbsp;
Besson revealed that he originally had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.800
Mel Gibson in mind for the role of Korben Dallas.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.320
"You shouldn't tempt me, man."

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.000
Besson wrote in the AMA:

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.360
"I had asked Mel Gibson first because he had his&nbsp;
office next to mine at Warner Brothers. He peeked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:37.360
into my office every morning to tell me that he&nbsp;
was thinking about it. After 3 months, he passed."

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.720
While it would have been fascinating to envision&nbsp;
Gibson as a futuristic cab driver who helps save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:46.480
the human race, it's difficult to deny just&nbsp;
how perfect Bruce Willis is in the role. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.320
a retrospective chat with Entertainment Weekly,&nbsp;
Besson revealed that he was initially hesitant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.640
reach out to the "Die Hard" actor because&nbsp;
of how costly his fees would be. Willis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.880
however, was immediately on board&nbsp;
with Besson's vision, saying,

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.240
"I just liked Luc — I liked the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.280
I liked the idea. I thought it would be&nbsp;
fun to go to France and make a movie."

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.680
And money ultimately didn't seem to be an issue&nbsp;
for Willis. As The Hollywood Reporter noted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.188
the "Sixth Sense" star took a pay&nbsp;
cut to work on "The Fifth Element."

00:02:13.235 --> 00:02:14.327
"You want a cup of coffee?"

00:02:14.327 --> 00:02:15.880
"Yes, please."

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:19.960
It's hard to imagine anyone else but Milla&nbsp;
Jovovich inhabiting the role of the mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:25.120
but joyful Leeloo. The heart of the film,&nbsp;
Jovovich expertly captures the raw intensity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.320
childlike wonder, and naivety that makes&nbsp;
Leeloo such a beloved character. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:33.520
for director Luc Besson, meeting Jovovich&nbsp;
was a boon — the two were briefly married&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:38.360
and reunited for the 1999 flick "The&nbsp;
Messenger: The Story of Joan of Arc."

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:43.080
Like most of the leading cast in "The Fifth&nbsp;
Element," Jovovich's casting was in fluctuation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.520
as several talented stars were vying for&nbsp;
the role. According to Jean Paul Gaultier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.200
Julia Roberts would have occupied the lead&nbsp;
role in an early iteration of "The Fifth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.364
Element." Unfortunately, Roberts, and a number of&nbsp;
other stars didn't get the "Fifth Element" gig.

00:02:56.364 --> 00:02:58.601
"Oh!”
[laughter]

00:02:58.601 --> 00:03:02.640
In a chat with Movieline, "Showgirls"&nbsp;
star Elizabeth Berkley revealed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.560
she was also in the running for a&nbsp;
role in "The Fifth Element," though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.680
things ultimately didn't work&nbsp;
out. Berkley told the outlet:

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.600
"I got very close to getting&nbsp;
cast on The Fifth Element,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:13.960
and Luc Besson couldn't&nbsp;
have been more supportive."

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:17.520
Ultimately, the cast of "The Fifth&nbsp;
Element" is perfect as it is. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.280
one can't help but wonder just how different&nbsp;
Besson's final product could have looked with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.600
Mel Gibson, Prince, or even Berkley&nbsp;
in the lead roles. Will we ever see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:30.480
the iconic cast return for a potential&nbsp;
sequel? It's unlikely. As Besson says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.000
he has no interest in revisiting his sci-fi&nbsp;
classic. The director told ComicBook.com:

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.600
"In 'The Fifth Element,' there's nothing in the&nbsp;
DNA to make a sequel. They just live together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.400
and they're happy, and she's the Fifth Element,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.739
and that's it. [...] It will be just&nbsp;
for money, and I'm not interested."

